{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הגוי גם צדיק תהרג", :translit "ha-goy gam tzadik teharog", :gloss "the nation also righteous will you kill"} {:he "פירוש אם תהרג אותי גם אברהם הצדיק תהרוג", :translit "pitarush im teharog oti gam avraham ha-tzadik teharog", :gloss "meaning if you kill me also Abraham the righteous you kill"} {:he "כי הוא פשע וגרם לי זאת", :translit "ki hu pasha ve-garam li zot", :gloss "for he sinned and caused me this"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Rereading Abimelech's plea as a legal argument: Abraham bears guilt for the deception, so killing Abimelech implicates Abraham too."}], :frame {:text "Abimelech protests to God, 'Shall You slay a righteous nation?' (Genesis 20:4). Abraham had instructed Sarah to say she was his sister (Genesis 20:2, cf. Genesis 12:13), setting in motion the events that led Abimelech to take her. Abimelech's plea builds on his claim of innocence: 'In the simplicity of my heart and the innocence of my hands have I done this' (Genesis 20:5).", :sources ["Genesis 12:13" "Genesis 20:2" "Genesis 20:4" "Genesis 20:5"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Rereading Abimelech's plea as a legal argument: Abraham bears guilt for the deception, so killing Abimelech implicates Abraham too.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:06:53.571529363Z", :model "glm-5.2"}